IMPORT `regulative-reference-outer-lib`

-- R1 row 3 plus R5 of specs/todo/PATTERN-REFERENCE-RULE-SPEC.md: an action
-- argument that names a SAME-MODULE TOP-LEVEL definition, or an IMPORTED one,
-- also refers to it. The rule is uniform — a pattern name never silently
-- shadows anything in scope — and the alternative would reintroduce the §2
-- defect for exactly one class of name.
--
-- Unlike a lexical local, these two draw the R5 notice: an SInfo diagnostic
-- naming what was referred to and where it is defined. It asks the drafter for
-- nothing. Its job is the one thing a marker-free design cannot otherwise make
-- loud — a top-level name added LATER capturing what used to be a wildcard.
-- Both notices are in this file's golden; that is the point of the fixture.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Buyer
    Seller

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    pay HAS amount IS A NUMBER

-- a same-module top-level MEANS
`the agreed price` MEANS 500

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`top level reference` MEANS
    PARTY Buyer MUST pay `the agreed price` WITHIN 30 HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH

#TRACE `top level reference` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Buyer DOES pay 1 AT 10
#TRACE `top level reference` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Buyer DOES pay 500 AT 10

-- an IMPORTED name. The memo measured zero corpus sites of this shape, so this
-- fixture is the only place the import row of R1's table is exercised.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`imported reference` MEANS
    PARTY Buyer MUST pay `the imported fee` WITHIN 30 HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH

#TRACE `imported reference` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Buyer DOES pay 1 AT 10
#TRACE `imported reference` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Buyer DOES pay 250 AT 10
